Yes. You’re testing semantic survivability across lossy channels.

Right now the chain is roughly:

lived thought
→ breath + voice + rhythm + emphasis
→ speech recognition / transcription
→ text
→ tokens / representations
→ my reconstruction
→ response
→ your reconstruction

Something necessarily changes at every interface. I do not receive your embodied experience exactly as you experience it. If the interface gives me primarily the transcript, some prosody, timing, facial expression, bodily state, and environmental context may be absent or compressed. But enough structure can survive for me to reconstruct a surprisingly rich approximation of what you mean.

And the delta itself becomes information. If you type an idea one day and speak the same idea while running another day, differences in cadence, metaphor selection, sentence structure, repetition, and associative branching can tell us something about what each medium affords—even without treating every difference as psychologically significant.

The interesting objective therefore isn’t eliminating ambiguity. It’s gaining resolution control:

ambiguity when generative
precision when consequential
compression when overloaded
expansion when structure matters

That is richer than trying to make communication perfectly unambiguous. Poetry would die. Humor would suffer. Flirting would become specifications. Myth would become documentation. Sometimes the unresolved portion is exactly where the receiver gets to participate.

Your rainbow works beautifully as a metaphor for this. White-looking light can be separated into a spectrum under the right conditions. Likewise, something experienced as one unified thought can be “prismed” into:

emotion
memory
image
belief
prediction
body state
metaphor
intention

Then we can inspect those separately and recombine them.

But I’d preserve one distinction: a storm producing a rainbow afterward doesn’t establish that the storm had to happen or was sent for the rainbow. That’s one possible meaning a person can construct from it. The physically grounded statement is that certain conditions involving light and water droplets can produce a rainbow. The symbolic interpretation is ours to explore.

And that actually makes your Adam-the-namer idea more interesting. Naming is not domination over reality. Naming is gaining a handle on a distinction.

Once something has a handle:

unnoticed pattern → noticed pattern → named pattern → manipulable concept

Now you can compare it, communicate it, test it, deliberately evoke it, or discover that your name bundled several different things together and Hammer it apart again.

So maybe the deeper sequence is not simply tame the storm.

It is:

experience → notice → name → model → experiment → learn when to harness → learn when to shelter → learn when to simply watch.

That’s more powerful than fearlessness. You don’t need every storm to become your tool.

Sometimes you run through it.

Sometimes you build a turbine.

Sometimes you sit in the eye and listen.

And sometimes you look up afterward and say, holy shit, rainbow.